1
00:00:00,300 --> 00:00:01,970
Tuesday morning, 09:15.

2
00:00:01,970 --> 00:00:09,310
You open your banking app and wire 12,000,000 VND for one full year of health and property insurance.

3
00:00:09,310 --> 00:00:14,250
Three seconds later, your checking balance drops by the full amount.

4
00:00:14,250 --> 00:00:25,360
Many buyers picture that cash resting inside a private vault with their name on the envelope, or jumping straight into the insurer's profit column today.

5
00:00:25,360 --> 00:00:26,520
Neither happens.

6
00:00:26,520 --> 00:00:37,120
Cash changes hands once, yet accounting revenue unlocks across twelve months while most reserves move straight into fixed-income markets all year.

7
00:00:37,700 --> 00:00:41,200
Start on the corporate buyer's ledger.

8
00:00:41,200 --> 00:00:53,810
Under Circular 99/2025/TT-BTC (unofficial summary), a firm paying 12,000,000 VND upfront cannot expense the entire policy inside January.

9
00:00:53,810 --> 00:01:02,830
Doing so would crush January operating margins while giving the remaining eleven months zero cost.

10
00:01:02,830 --> 00:01:14,800
Accounting therefore books Debit Account 242 Deferred expenses and Credit Account 112 Demand deposits for 12,000,000 VND on Day 1.

11
00:01:14,800 --> 00:01:24,090
At Month 1 close, one-twelfth amortizes via Debit Account 642 / Credit Account 242 for 1,000,000 VND.

12
00:01:24,670 --> 00:01:27,170
Now step inside the insurer's door.

13
00:01:27,170 --> 00:01:36,600
No standalone contract works for a single buyer because one severe surgery or fire easily wipes out a small 12-million contribution.

14
00:01:36,600 --> 00:01:40,240
The entire model rests on the law of large numbers.

15
00:01:40,240 --> 00:01:45,810
Suppose 1,000 people all wire 12,000,000 VND during the first week of January.

16
00:01:45,810 --> 00:01:58,980
Together those deposits form one shared 12,000,000,000 VND (12 billion VND) pool in the collecting bank, turning unpredictable personal shocks into a fairly steady annual payout curve.

17
00:01:59,560 --> 00:02:04,160
Yet all 12 billion VND does not sit untouched inside claims reserves.

18
00:02:04,160 --> 00:02:09,300
Selling policies and vetting medical files costs real cash upfront in Week 1.

19
00:02:09,300 --> 00:02:19,250
In this illustrative example, roughly 25% of collected premiums — 3,000,000,000 VND total, or 3,000,000 VND per contract — exits the pool right away.

20
00:02:19,250 --> 00:02:28,000
Two billion pays distribution commissions (Account 641), while one billion covers underwriting and IT administration (Account 642).

21
00:02:28,000 --> 00:02:35,760
Only nine billion VND stays inside to back risk, which is why early cancellation always triggers upfront deductions.

22
00:02:36,340 --> 00:02:39,260
Rule four lives on the income statement.

23
00:02:39,260 --> 00:02:50,210
Even with all 12,000,000 VND already cleared into bank cash on Tuesday morning, accounting prohibits recognizing the full lump sum in January revenue.

24
00:02:50,210 --> 00:02:54,590
Only 30 days of protection have elapsed when Month 1 closes.

25
00:02:54,590 --> 00:03:00,570
So just one-twelfth — 1,000,000 VND per contract — counts as earned revenue today.

26
00:03:00,570 --> 00:03:09,390
The remaining 11,000,000 VND stays locked as an unearned liability backing the eleven months still ahead on the calendar.

27
00:03:09,970 --> 00:03:21,790
Look at the double-entry balance under our general Circular 99/2025 enterprise reference (unofficial summary), running parallel to IFRS 17.

28
00:03:21,790 --> 00:03:33,690
While licensed Vietnamese insurers use dedicated statutory insurance sub-accounts, general corporate books make the economics crystal clear.

29
00:03:33,690 --> 00:03:49,160
Month 1 pairs Debit Account 112 (12,000,000 VND cash in) against Credit Account 3387 Unearned revenue (11,000,000 VND) and Credit Account 511 Sales & service revenue (1,000,000 VND).

30
00:03:49,160 --> 00:03:52,650
IFRS 17 labels that liability bucket LRC.

31
00:03:52,650 --> 00:03:57,150
Cash arrives on Day 1; profit unlocks month by month.

32
00:03:57,730 --> 00:04:02,580
Where do the surviving 9 billion VND of reserves stay all year?

33
00:04:02,580 --> 00:04:06,430
Not inside near-zero ~0.1%/year checking accounts.

34
00:04:06,430 --> 00:04:17,430
Because hospital bills and property repairs land unevenly from February through December, treasury splits the 9B pool into two distinct layers.

35
00:04:17,430 --> 00:04:23,580
One billion stays liquid inside checking Account 112 to fund weekly claims fast.

36
00:04:23,580 --> 00:04:40,660
Meanwhile 8 billion VND shifts immediately into Account 128 Held-to-maturity investments, prioritizing government bonds and laddered bank term deposits that earn predictable coupons without locking cash into illiquid bets.

37
00:04:41,240 --> 00:04:47,550
That 8B bond bucket reveals the core engine of insurance finance: float.

38
00:04:47,550 --> 00:05:01,050
Suppose covered claims consume 9,000,000,000 VND across the cohort (a 75% loss ratio), plus 3,000,000,000 VND in upfront commissions and operations (25%).

39
00:05:01,050 --> 00:05:08,590
Combined payouts equal 100% of collected premiums, producing zero underwriting profit.

40
00:05:08,590 --> 00:05:25,770
Still, holding 8,000,000,000 VND of insurance float inside Account 128 at an illustrative 6%/year yield generates 480,000,000 VND (480,000 VND per policy) straight into Account 515 Finance income.

41
00:05:26,350 --> 00:05:39,290
When someone in the 1,000-policy pool suffers a covered injury or fire, claims awaiting final invoice audit cannot stay off the books.

42
00:05:39,290 --> 00:05:51,560
Accounting immediately logs a claims reserve, shown on Account 352 Provisions under Circular 99/2025 (parallel to IFRS 17 LIC).

43
00:05:51,560 --> 00:06:10,980
Once approved, payouts wire out from the 1B Account 112 buffer via Debit Account 352 / Credit Account 112 so claimants get cash quickly, while maturing Account 128 bonds refill liquidity every quarter.

44
00:06:11,560 --> 00:06:15,300
Three everyday scenarios close the 365-day loop.

45
00:06:15,300 --> 00:06:31,900
First, zero claims over twelve months means Account 3387 empties gradually into Account 511 with no year-end refund on pure protection; your 12M helped shield hurt cohort members while buying continuous certainty.

46
00:06:31,900 --> 00:06:40,550
Second, canceling mid-year returns only the unelapsed time slice minus upfront acquisition costs already spent.

47
00:06:40,550 --> 00:06:52,540
Third, severe disasters pushing the combined ratio above 100% draw on Account 515 investment returns, statutory reserves, and excess reinsurance treaties.
